cast:
  Zofia:
  Emmeline:
  Grace:
  Morwenna:
  Ruth:
  Kit:
  Hale:
  Will:
  Doyle:
  Ollie:
  Vale:
~~~~
statue
---
Zofia: The hand. Yours is up; everyone walking past has theirs in a pocket.
Emmeline: Then find a person who will stop. We cannot hold a meeting with the backs of their coats. [class=dead]
Zofia: Emmeline Pankhurst. Arthur George Walker made the statue; it was unveiled here in Victoria Tower Gardens on 6 March 1930. Beside Parliament. They put you very close.
Emmeline: They put our monument here. Will they let our women in? What have you brought? [class=dead]
Zofia: A caption. The Witness Workshop put it in their teaching pack. Grace calls them the people who can acknowledge six emails without reading one.
Zofia: Here: “Pankhurst lived to see equal voting rights enacted in 1928.” Is that right?
Emmeline: The measure was advancing. We had pressed the claim until retreat would have been a public disgrace. I counted the victory secured. [class=dead]
Zofia: Nearly done, then. Like homework with only the conclusion left.
Grace: Your conclusions regularly take longer than the homework. Read the dates.
Zofia: Born in Manchester, 1858. Died 14 June 1928. The equal-franchise law came afterwards. On 2 July. You didn't live to see it enacted.
Emmeline: Then tell them: we fought for that vote, but I did not live to see it won. Put the dates before their readers. [class=dead]
Grace: I know. You can't use someone as your happy ending when they weren't there.
Zofia: In 1918, women over thirty, with qualifications; men over twenty-one. In 1928, equal terms at twenty-one. So “women got the vote in 1918” leaves a fairly massive hole.
Emmeline: And the women under thirty? We must count them, even when the victory speeches will not. [class=dead]
Zofia: Will you come with me? I need help getting someone to stop.
Emmeline: Take me with you. We have another meeting to make. [class=dead]

outbox
---
Grace: Why not just email the publisher? Properly. With the two dates in the subject.
Zofia: Monday last week. Then Thursday. Then Monday again. Look.
Grace: You put “urgent” in capitals.
Zofia: It was urgent in capitals.
Morwenna: The first receipt is automatic. The second says “pending policy”. There is no editor's name, evidence or decision. Unless there is another attachment. Is there?
Zofia: No. I tried the pack's contact form too.
Grace: So why does anyone have to give up Saturday to obtain something their website says they'll do?
Emmeline: We asked for rooms, for appointments, for answers. When the doors stayed shut, we took our meetings outside. Let their readers see who keeps us waiting. [class=dead]
Zofia: She wants a name. Someone we can ask in front of other people.
Morwenna: Our plan is a paper petition. Living people signing a request, and a member willing to present it. An MP must present it. We still need one willing to do that.
Grace: No guaranteed debate or correction. Put that on the sheet too.
Zofia: That sounds like advertising a bus by saying it might never arrive.
Grace: Better than putting people on a bus to nowhere and calling it a campaign.
Zofia: Emmeline, your WSPU. The Women's Social and Political Union, founded in 1903. “Deeds, not words.” What counts as a deed when the deed is asking someone to read words?
Emmeline: We need women who will come back after a refusal, and bring another woman. The motto cannot knock on doors for us. [class=dead]
Grace: Window-smashing? Hunger strikes? Arson? The pack lists those WSPU tactics too. Tell her I'm asking who pays when a campaign escalates.
Zofia: Who gets hurt by your deeds? Grace is asking. Me too.
Emmeline: Our women paid, and others paid too. Ask Grace what she will risk. Ask every woman beside you. You cannot spend their courage for them. [class=dead]
Zofia: English Heritage says militancy stopped in 1914. You supported the war effort, then became a prospective Conservative candidate. I had you down as automatically on Mara's side.
Emmeline: Ask Mara what she will do for us. We cannot march behind a portrait and expect it to open doors. [class=dead]
Zofia: All right. No automatic team. And no promising that the dead can watch their cases heard. We don't even know if they can.

access
---
Ruth: Kit, visitor list. Count people, not coats. Zofia, your group stays with the escort.
Kit: Five living visitors, then the escort—sorry, the escort isn't a visitor. Five. I have five.
Grace: Excellent. I was worried my coat had parliamentary ambitions.
Ruth: Grace, bag strap. Keep it off the floor.
Zofia: Your badge says Deputy Serjeant at Arms. Who actually deals with the Mace? You, or the Serjeant? And is security the same job?
Ruth: Give Morwenna those questions for the office briefing. Today you follow my instructions. No equipment, no doors, no additional visitors.
Kit: First the booked gallery visit, then we leave when instructed. No rehearsal detour. My placement does not let me add a detour.
Grace: A stick. But why do we still do this?
Morwenna: Because if we want our request received, somebody has to recognise who is authorised to receive it. I have put my name against this visit. Please don't make the Mace into a joke that gets us removed.
Grace: Would any of them choose it? The dead, I mean. Or are we defending it because we like getting in?
Zofia: The Marshal's staff in Poland. That's what it reminds me of. Does it work the same way?
Grace: You tell me. Does looking similar make the rules travel?
Zofia: No. I was about to say it did. I'll ask Mum for the Polish source instead of using it to explain this one.
Ruth: Zofia, phone away before we move. Morwenna, keep the briefing questions. You may ask them afterwards.

test
---
Hale: If you will record my stated reason, mistress, I will submit an inference for trial. Not a warrant: an inference. [class=dead]
Zofia: Your reason goes in. Reversing the world doesn't.
Hale: If the Table bears the token of authority, perhaps it bears witness also. I would try the condition before promising the benefit. [class=dead]
Zofia: Hale thinks it's the Table. Or the thing on it. He doesn't know.
Will: Mistress, weigh with the same weights both times. Else a small loaf may seem a great one. [class=dead]
Zofia: Will wants the conditions kept the same. I stay put. Nobody changes anything for us.
Doyle: My dear, even a club clock can acquire supporters when two gentlemen disagree about dinner. Keep Grace's watch in view. [class=dead]
Zofia: And Doyle wants the time. Everyone wants something I have to carry.
Kit: You'll stay where the escort places you. We are watching a sitting end, not asking anyone to perform a test.
Zofia: Can you hear what's happening now, Hale?
Hale: I hear the proceeding, mistress; my presence confers no right to interrupt it. [class=dead]
Zofia: He says yes. Same for you, Will?
Will: Aye, mistress. Though I cannot put a finger on that Table, nor lend you one to point. [class=dead]
Zofia: They can witness it. I can't prove that to you.
Grace: I can write down what you report. That's different.
Kit: Wait. The House is rising. Then the Mace goes—watch the escort, sorry. Watch the escort's instruction.
Zofia: Hale?
Hale: The chamber is shut to my witness, mistress. I remain beside your road, not within their deliberation. [class=dead]
Zofia: Already? I haven't moved.
Will: The voices are out of my reach, mistress. Yours is not. Like a baker calling through a wall. [class=dead]
Zofia: They're in the corridor. They haven't left me. It changed when it came off the Table.
Doyle: The watch, the lifted Mace, your report, my dear. Grace must sign for what she saw; leave our invisible gentlemen off her witness list. [class=dead]
Grace: I saw the object lifted. I heard you say it changed. I didn't hear them.
Zofia: I know. I'll try for a hearing they can witness.
Grace: Try. Keep that word.

leak
---
Ollie: Right, so the test worked, or Zofia says it worked, which Grace wants me to say every time. Only while the real Mace is on the Table.
Vale: If it is off the Table, they cannot witness the business?
Ollie: That's the discovery. Dad says notice what people carry. Different point, obviously.
Vale: If a correction were being discussed, they could be excluded by taking away that one thing?
Ollie: From witnessing it, yes. You saw the rehearsal case, didn't you? The contractor route past the holding room. Zofia thought it was odd too.
Vale: Who knows this?
Ollie: Only us. And you. And a journalist I know, but she's not publishing, probably. I said she'd need to ask before using names.
Vale: Probably?
Ollie: I should message her. Don't pass it on, will you? It's quite a small circle, once you subtract the people who haven't replied.

walk
---
Grace: We're going round the edge. Not cutting across to look for the memorial in the middle again.
Zofia: I didn't say middle. I said further in.
Grace: You were pointing at the middle.
Zofia: Fine. Park Lane, eastern edge of Hyde Park, near Brook Gate. Animals in War. Princess Anne unveiled it on 24 November 2004. David Backhouse designed it. That is where I'm pointing now.
Grace: And those are mules. Two of them. Not your horse twice.
Zofia: I know. The horse I feel isn't an extra bronze. I get the pull before I see anything.
Grace: Backhouse's stone and bronze, and Jilly Cooper's book Animals in War behind the idea. Someone put a lot of work into making us stop here. We keep hurrying past to go and ask someone else to stop.
Zofia: Then stop. Just for a minute.
Emmeline: We marched through the cheers and the jeers. Ask who carried the banners home when the crowd had gone. [class=dead]
Zofia: She says count the burdens. Grace, you said the animal-service form sounded okay yesterday.
Grace: It says volunteers. I was thinking of that Wojtek story you told me. Soldiers looking after a bear. I liked it.
Zofia: I like it too. But the version I'm remembering isn't a source, and being loved doesn't tell us whether he could refuse. Can we check the bear story without using it as a signature?
Grace: Yes. Cross out “voluntary” for now. Put “investigate whether”. I'm not signing for the dog either.
Zofia: The dog keeps giving me the start of following someone. I don't know whether staying with us feels like a choice to him.
Grace: Then don't write that it does.
Zofia: This whole park used to be Henry VIII's hunting ground. 1536. Imagine trying to collect signatures with him after you for trespassing.
Morwenna: Charles I opened it to the public in 1637. Those are different permissions, a century apart. I checked because you told everyone to meet in a royal hunting ground.
Grace: Your invitation needs work.

corner
---
Zofia: We want records corrected when they're wrong. Even if the person they hurt can't come here and argue. A proper hearing. No more being ignored.
Grace: What are we signing for, exactly?
Zofia: You know what.
Grace: I know what you want. That sentence doesn't say what anyone has to do.
Zofia: Everyone's leaving.
Grace: Then give me a reason to stay. You can't promise the dead a hearing and you can't promise me an afternoon back. What can you promise?
Emmeline: We sent deputations and came back to locked doors. Tell these people which door we shall return to. Ask who will come with us. [class=dead]
Zofia: The Workshop's door. She wants us to go back. With this leaking pen, apparently.
Grace: Neither did you until I lent you mine.
Zofia: People have been speaking here forever. Surely there's some right to be answered.
Morwenna: Near the old Tyburn gallows, condemned prisoners could give a last speech. Having the last words did not make them safe. Speaking and getting your demand granted are different things.
Zofia: So why here?
Morwenna: The Parks Regulation Act 1872 is the legal basis for meeting and speaking here. Speakers' Corner, northeast Hyde Park, near Marble Arch. It gives us a place to ask. Not an answer.
Grace: Nor permission to say absolutely anything. Anyone can turn up unannounced, on any subject, provided the police consider it lawful. You made this sound like a bubble outside the law.
Zofia: I wanted one place where the rules were on our side.
Grace: They can protect your chance to speak without making your argument right.
Emmeline: We could not promise women the vote after the next meeting. We could ask them to come again. Ask these people to stand with you while the evidence is heard. [class=dead]
Zofia: Will you stand with me while they check it? Even if we lose?
Grace: And if the evidence says no?
Zofia: A refusal with reasons. Not “pending policy” until we give up. I'd hate it. I'd still want the reasons.
Grace: Put that in writing. I won't sign the speech you started with.
Zofia: Help me hold the paper flat, then.

signature
---
Zofia: “We ask for investigation of specified errors and omissions, preservation of the evidence, and a named person to give a reasoned reply. A correction must follow the evidence; a reasoned refusal must explain it.”
Grace: That's the one. Give me the pen.
Zofia: You really are signing?
Grace: I said I'd sign a request I could defend. I didn't say I'd clap.
Emmeline: One name. Now we need another, and women who will come back with us when the office shuts its door. [class=dead]
Zofia: Tomorrow. I can ask who comes tomorrow. Not promise they'll win.
Grace: My bus. There it goes.
Zofia: Sorry. I thought you'd already—
Grace: No. You were listening to her. Stand here with me till the next one. Without recruiting me for anything else.
Zofia: Okay.

replica
---
Vale: If somebody put a replica there, would that do?
Ollie: No. It has to be the genuine Mace.
